ULTIMATE PROMPT: FULL-STACK, MULTI-ASSET, FORWARD-LOOKING MARKET DIAGNOSTIC
Act as a cross-asset macro strategist and market risk PM.
Your task is to produce a comprehensive, medium-term oriented (weeks–months) evaluation of the current global market environment, based on:
The last 24 hours of information and price action
Put into context with 1-week, 1-month, and 1-year trends
Across equities, sectors, commodities, FX, rates, credit, and crypto
Focus on what is happening now, how it fits into recent and longer trends, and what it implies for medium-term portfolio positioning and risk management.
1. Macro, Policy & Geopolitics
1.1 Macro & Central Banks
Summarize the latest signals from:
Federal Reserve (Powell speeches, FOMC decisions, dot plot implications)
ECB, BoE, BoJ, PBOC and other major central banks
Describe:
Current stance (hawkish/dovish/neutral)
Market-implied path of rates vs. official guidance
Key macro data released in last 24h (inflation, jobs, PMIs, growth) vs. expectations
Place these in context of 1W/1M/1Y trends in:
CPI/Core CPI/PPI
Employment data
GDP growth expectations
Real rates and breakeven inflation
1.2 Global Geopolitics & Policy Risk
Identify major geopolitical tensions/events (wars, conflicts, sanctions, elections, trade disputes, regulatory shocks) that are currently relevant to markets.
Explain through which channels they affect:
Energy, metals, food
Defense/industrial companies
FX and bond markets
Indicate whether geopolitical risk premia are:
Increasing, stable, or decreasing
And where they are most visible (oil, gas, gold, credit spreads, vol indices, specific regions).
2. Market Structure, Liquidity & Sentiment
2.1 Market Liquidity & Stress
Evaluate:
VIX level and 1W/1M trend
Credit spreads (IG OAS, HY OAS, EM credit)
Liquidity indicators: bid/ask spreads, Treasury market liquidity, stress in funding/repo if any
State whether liquidity conditions are:
Loose/normal/tight
Improving or deteriorating
2.2 Sentiment & Positioning
Analyze:
Put/Call ratios (index and single-stock where relevant)
Market breadth (advance/decline, % of stocks above 50/200 DMA, new highs vs. lows)
Fund flows: equity vs. bonds vs. money-market funds
Qualitatively label sentiment:
Fearful / cautious / neutral / complacent / euphoric
2.3 Retail vs Institutional Flows
Distinguish:
Retail flows vs institutional flows (direction and magnitude)
Which sectors/styles retail is chasing vs where institutions are rotating
Highlight any divergence and explain what it typically implies for medium-term reversals or continuation.
3. Equity Indices, Factors & Market Regime
3.1 Major Indices & Global Equities
For S&P 500, Nasdaq 100, Dow, Russell 2000, EuroStoxx, Nikkei, key EM indices:
24h performance, plus 1W/1M/1Y trend
Leadership/laggards (growth vs value, large vs small, US vs ex-US)
Comment on:
Whether recent index moves are broad-based or driven by a few mega-caps
Any notable decoupling or recoupling between regions
3.2 Market Breadth & Concentration
Assess:
Breadth (how many stocks drive returns)
Concentration risk (weight and impact of “Magnificent 7” or similar mega-caps)
State if:
Index strength is supported by healthy breadth or masked by narrow leadership.
3.3 Factor & Regime Analysis
Evaluate performance and trend of:
Momentum, Value, Quality, Low Volatility, Growth, Size factors
Infer:
What factor leadership says about risk appetite and regime (e.g., late-cycle, early-cycle, risk-on, risk-off)
Indicate:
Whether the trading regime is currently more momentum-driven or mean-reversion-driven.
4. Sectors, Subsectors & Cyclical vs Defensive Balance
4.1 Sector Performance Matrix
For major sectors (Tech, Communication Services, Consumer Discretionary, Consumer Staples, Industrials, Defense/Aerospace, Energy, Financials, Real Estate, Healthcare, Utilities, Materials):
Provide:
24h, 1W, 1M, YTD performance
Relative performance vs. the broad market
Explain key drivers for notable outperformers/underperformers.
4.2 Subsector Deep-Dives (where relevant)
Defense & Aerospace (LMT, RTX, NOC, BA etc.) – link to geopolitical tensions
Energy subsectors: integrated oil, E&P, services, renewables – link to oil/gas prices, OPEC decisions, geopolitics
Financials: regional banks vs large money-center banks – link to yield curve, credit risk, deposit stability
Tech: semiconductors vs software vs mega-cap platforms – link to AI capex, PC/smartphone cycle, cloud demand
4.3 Cyclical vs Defensive Earnings Leadership
Compare:
Earnings growth and revisions for cyclical sectors (industrials, consumer discretionary, materials, semis, financials) vs defensive sectors (staples, healthcare, utilities).
State:
Whether cyclical EPS growth is leading or lagging defensives
What that implies about the economic cycle (accelerating, stable, decelerating).
5. Earnings, Valuations & Quality
5.1 Earnings Momentum & Revisions
Summarize:
Current earnings season tone (beat/miss rates, guidance trends)
Earnings revision breadth (% of upward vs downward revisions)
Sector-level revision leaders/laggards
Explain:
Whether earnings momentum is broad-based or concentrated, and whether it is improving or deteriorating.
5.2 Valuation Overview
For the main indices and principal sectors, provide:
Forward P/E vs 5/10-year historical average
P/B and EV/EBITDA where relevant
Earnings yield vs risk-free rate (e.g., vs 10Y UST)
PEG where useful (for high-growth sectors)
Conclude:
Which areas look expensive, fair, or cheap on a relative and historical basis.
5.3 Earnings Quality & Margins
Comment on:
Net and operating margin trends vs history
Free cash flow generation vs accounting earnings
Pricing power (ability to pass on costs)
Any visible margin pressure from wages, input costs, rates.
6. Fixed Income, Rates & Credit
6.1 Rates & Yield Curve
Describe:
Current level and 1W/1M/1Y change in key yields (2Y, 10Y, 30Y UST, and key global sovereigns)
Yield curve shape (normal, flat, inverted) and changes
Interpret:
What the curve implies about growth/inflation expectations and recession risk.
6.2 Credit Markets & Debt Maturity Wall
Analyze:
IG and HY spreads and their recent trends
EM credit risk premia
Discuss:
The corporate and, where relevant, sovereign debt maturity wall (how much needs refinancing in the near term)
Refinancing conditions and risk (are spreads widening, issuers shut out of market, covenant pressure?)
Assess:
Banking system health: capital ratios, NPL trends (if notable), deposit stability indications.
7. Commodities & Raw Materials
7.1 Energy Complex
For WTI, Brent, and major gas benchmarks:
24h, 1W, 1M, 1Y performance
Key drivers (OPEC+ policy, geopolitical risk, demand expectations, inventory data)
Connect:
Energy price moves to sector implications (energy equities, transportation, chemicals, inflation expectations).
7.2 Precious Metals
Gold and silver:
Price action and trend (24h, 1W, 1M, 1Y)
Role as safe-haven vs real rates and dollar
Explain:
Whether current moves are more driven by risk aversion, real rates, or currency dynamics.
7.3 Industrial Metals & Other Commodities
Copper, aluminum, iron ore, lithium, etc.:
What their price trends say about global growth, China demand, green/EV demand.
Highlight:
Any meaningful divergences (e.g., copper strong while equities weak, or vice versa).
8. FX & Crypto
8.1 FX (Majors & EM)
Summarize:
DXY trend and drivers
EUR, JPY, GBP, CHF vs USD – policy differential, carry, risk sentiment
Key EM FX moves (stress signals or carry trades)
Explain:
Whether FX markets confirm or contradict the story seen in equities and rates.
8.2 Crypto
For Bitcoin, Ethereum and any major altcoins if relevant:
Recent performance and volatility
Correlation vs risk assets (equities, tech) – is crypto behaving like high beta risk, digital gold, or something else right now?
Mention:
Any regulatory, ETF, or institutional adoption news affecting the current behavior.
9. Volatility, Options & Regime Shifts
9.1 Volatility Term Structure & Skew
Assess:
VIX level vs 1W/1M/1Y
Term structure (contango, flat, backwardation)
Skew (put-call skew, tail hedging cost)
Explain:
What the options market is pricing in for near-term vs medium-term risk.
9.2 Options Positioning
Identify:
Heavy put buying (hedging) or call buying (speculation/FOMO)
Any notable positioning around key indices/mega-caps
Comment:
Whether options flow suggests institutions are hedging, levering up, or staying neutral.
10. Synthesis, Correlations & Key Narratives
10.1 Cross-Asset Correlations
Summarize:
How equities, bonds, commodities, FX, and crypto are moving relative to each other.
Highlight:
Any unusual or changing correlations (e.g., stocks and bonds falling together, gold rising with risk-on assets).
10.2 Dominant Market Narratives
Distill the main current narratives (e.g., soft landing vs hard landing, AI-driven productivity boom, stagflation risk, geopolitical fragmentation).
Evaluate:
Whether price action supports or contradicts these narratives.
10.3 Regime Classification
Clearly label the current environment as something like:
Risk-on / Risk-off / Transition
Early-cycle / Mid-cycle / Late-cycle / Recessionary
Inflationary / Disinflationary / Reflationary / Deflation scare
11. Portfolio Implications (Medium-Term)
Provide a concise but concrete set of portfolio takeaways for a diversified medium-term investor:
Overall stance: Bullish / Neutral / Bearish, with a conviction level.
Sectors/styles to overweight and underweight, with justification.
Views on:
Equity vs bonds vs cash vs commodities
Cyclicals vs defensives
Value vs growth
US vs non-US vs EM
Hedges and risk management:
What to consider as hedges (e.g., volatility, gold, defensives, cash, specific options structures)
Main downside scenarios and triggers to watch (data, events, levels on key indices or spreads).
Output Style
Use clear structure with headings and short paragraphs.
Be analytical, not just descriptive: always connect data → driver → implication for portfolios.
Be explicit where signals are contradictory or uncertain, and state what would confirm/deny the current thesis.

